      <main class="docs-main">
        <section class="docs-hero">
          <h2 class="docs-hero__heading">OpenCastle for Teams</h2>
          <p class="docs-hero__sub">
            One source for every assistant, shared across every repository you own: a
            baseline your repositories extend, a policy for the MCP servers your agents
            may start, a lock file that records what every assistant is given, and a
            review that says what each change does.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════ The problem ═══════════ -->
        <section class="docs-section" id="problem">
          <h3 class="section-title">The Problem</h3>
          <p>
            A team runs several assistants side by side, and each reads its own format.
            Without one source, nobody reviews what the agents are told, nobody notices
            when one repository's rules drift from another's, and nobody can say which
            MCP servers — code that runs with every developer's credentials — the agents
            actually start.
          </p>
          <p>
            OpenCastle compiles one source into the native format of seven assistants.
            For teams it adds four things:
          </p>
          <ul class="docs-list">
            <li><strong>Baselines</strong> — your organisation's instructions, skills, agents, MCP servers and policy in an npm package that every repository extends. Your package manager fetches and pins it; your upgrade bot rolls it out.</li>
            <li><strong>Policy</strong> — which MCP servers may run, which hosts remote ones may reach, whether they must be pinned, which items no repository may drop, and how much context may load before a task. It only tightens.</li>
            <li><strong>The lock</strong> — <code>.opencastle/lock.json</code>, one reviewable record of what every assistant is given, checked in CI.</li>
            <li><strong>Seven commands</strong> — <code>explain</code> for onboarding, <code>review</code> for pull requests, <code>ci</code> to wire it up, <code>baseline</code> and <code>plugin</code> to publish a standard, <code>promote</code> to make what one person's assistant learned the team's, <code>fleet</code> to see where it has landed.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════ Layers ═══════════ -->
        <section class="docs-section" id="layers">
          <h3 class="section-title">Layers</h3>
          <p>
            What the assistants in a repository receive is built from layers. Each layer is a
            directory laid out the same way, and later layers add to and override earlier ones.
          </p>

          <div class="architecture-card">
            <div class="architecture-card__header">Layers, bottom to top</div>
            <div class="architecture-card__flow">
              <div class="architecture-card__node">OpenCastle — its own agents, skills, instructions, prompts and workflows, and the integrations your stack uses</div>
              <div class="architecture-card__arrow">then each baseline in <code>extends</code>, in order</div>
              <div class="architecture-card__node">@acme/opencastle-baseline — an Agent Plugin: its <code>skills/</code>, <code>mcp.json</code> and <code>dev.opencastle/</code></div>
              <div class="architecture-card__arrow">then the repository itself</div>
              <div class="architecture-card__node architecture-card__node--lead">This repository — <code>.opencastle/</code></div>
              <div class="architecture-card__arrow">compiled into every target</div>
              <div class="architecture-card__row">
                <div class="architecture-card__node">Claude Code</div>
                <div class="architecture-card__node">Copilot</div>
                <div class="architecture-card__node">Cursor</div>
                <div class="architecture-card__node">Windsurf</div>
                <div class="architecture-card__node">OpenCode</div>
                <div class="architecture-card__node">Codex CLI</div>
                <div class="architecture-card__node">Antigravity</div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>A layer holds any of these:</p>
          <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
            <table class="comparison-table">
              <thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>config.json</code></td><td><code>extends</code>, <code>exclude</code>, <code>mcpServers</code>, <code>policy</code> and <code>opencastle</code> — see the <a class="docs-link" href="#config">config reference</a></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>instructions/*.md</code></td><td>Rules every assistant reads before every task</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'skills/<name>/SKILL.md'}</code></td><td>Knowledge an assistant loads when a task matches the skill's <code>description</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>agents/*.agent.md</code></td><td>Personas to delegate to</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>prompts/*.md</code></td><td>Reusable prompts, compiled as <code>/oc:&lt;name&gt;</code> commands in Claude Code and Copilot</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>workflows/*.md</code></td><td>Multi-step workflow templates</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            In a repository the layer is <code>.opencastle/</code>. In a baseline it is the
            directory the package names in its <code>package.json</code>, such as
            <code>{'"opencastle": { "baseline": "." }'}</code>. Only <code>config.json</code>
            and the five content directories are read as a layer; the rest of
            <code>.opencastle/</code> — stack notes, lessons, the manifest — is not part of it.
          </p>
          <p>
            A baseline that is an <a href="/docs/agent-plugins">Agent Plugin</a> — one with a
            <code>plugin.json</code> at its root, as <code>baseline init</code> creates — keeps its skills in
            the plugin's <code>skills/</code>, servers any assistant can start as they are in
            <code>mcp.json</code>, and everything else, <code>config.json</code> included, in
            <code>dev.opencastle/</code>. Assistants that read Agent Plugins can then install it as it is. Any
            Agent Plugin on npm can be extended the same way, with no OpenCastle declaration. A baseline laid out
            the earlier way, with the layer's directories at its root, still works.
          </p>
          <p>
            Items are named by kind and name: <code>skills/deploy-runbook</code>,
            <code>instructions/security</code>, <code>agents/reviewer</code>. An item with the
            same name as one below replaces it whole — a skill's directory is not merged file by
            file. <code>exclude</code> removes an item from below. <code>sync</code> fills in
            what each assistant's format needs: an instruction that does not set <code>applyTo</code>
            is given <code>{"applyTo: '**'"}</code>, so Copilot loads it everywhere as the other assistants
            do. Line endings are normalised and OS files (<code>.DS_Store</code>, <code>Thumbs.db</code>) are skipped,
            so the lock comes out the same on every machine. A link to somewhere outside both the layer and the
            project is not followed, with a warning saying so. A
            skill or agent with no <code>description</code> compiles with a warning: no
            assistant can tell when to load a skill that does not describe itself.
          </p>
          <div class="key-insight">
            <p><strong>Policy runs the other way.</strong> Content from a higher layer wins; rules from a lower layer hold. A repository can add to its baseline's policy and make it stricter, but nothing it writes loosens what the baseline set.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <div class="section-divider">
          <span class="section-divider__line"></span>
          <span class="section-divider__label">Walkthrough</span>
          <span class="section-divider__line"></span>
        </div>

        <!-- ═══════════ Worked example ═══════════ -->
        <section class="docs-section" id="example">
          <h3 class="section-title">Worked Example</h3>
          <p>
            Acme publishes a baseline, <code>@acme/opencastle-baseline</code>. A repository called
            <em>shop</em> extends it, adds a skill of its own and two MCP servers. The output below
            comes from that setup, with long lists trimmed.
          </p>

          <h4 class="arch-subheading">1. The baseline package</h4>
          <pre><code>{baselineTree}</code></pre>
          <pre><code>{baselineConfig}</code></pre>
          <p>
            Two always-loaded instructions and a skill, one remote MCP server for every repository,
            and a policy: six allowed server names, one of them a wildcard for Acme's own servers; two
            hosts; exact versions; two items no repository may drop; and a budget of 12,000 tokens
            loaded before each task.
          </p>

          <h4 class="arch-subheading">2. The repository</h4>
          <p>
            <em>shop</em> installs the baseline as a devDependency — <code>npm i -D @acme/opencastle-baseline</code> —
            so the version lives in <code>package.json</code> and the lockfile pins it. Then it extends it:
          </p>
          <pre><code>{projectConfig}</code></pre>
          <p>
            It also has a skill of its own, <code>.opencastle/skills/deploy-runbook/SKILL.md</code>, and
            leaves out an OpenCastle skill it has no use for. <code>npx opencastle sync</code> compiles all of it:
          </p>
          <pre><code>{syncOutput}</code></pre>

          <h4 class="arch-subheading">3. Each assistant gets its own spelling</h4>
          <p>
            The variable is written once, as <code>{'${ACME_DOCS_TOKEN}'}</code>. There is no syntax every
            assistant expands, so each receives its own:
          </p>
          <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
            <table class="comparison-table">
              <thead><tr><th>Assistant</th><th>File</th><th>The header it receives</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Claude Code</td><td><code>.mcp.json</code></td><td><code>{'Bearer ${ACME_DOCS_TOKEN}'}</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Cursor, Windsurf</td><td><code>.cursor/mcp.json</code>, <code>.windsurf/mcp.json</code></td><td><code>{'Bearer ${env:ACME_DOCS_TOKEN}'}</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>OpenCode</td><td><code>opencode.json</code></td><td><code>{'Bearer {env:ACME_DOCS_TOKEN}'}</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>VS Code</td><td><code>.vscode/mcp.json</code></td><td><code>{'Bearer ${input:ACME_DOCS_TOKEN}'}</code> — a password input VS Code asks for once and keeps</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Codex CLI</td><td><code>.codex/config.toml</code></td><td>None written as text: Codex expands no variables, so the server gets <code>bearer_token_env_var = "ACME_DOCS_TOKEN"</code> and Codex sends the header itself</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Antigravity</td><td><code>.agents/mcp_config.json</code></td><td><code>{'Bearer ${ACME_DOCS_TOKEN}'}</code>, until its syntax is confirmed</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            For <code>acme-db</code>, whose <code>env</code> only passes <code>ACME_DB_URL</code> through,
            VS Code gets <code>{'"envFile": "${workspaceFolder}/.env"'}</code> instead of a prompt. Editor
            variables are not environment variables: VS Code and Cursor get <code>{'${workspaceFolder}'}</code> and
            <code>{'${userHome}'}</code> as written; Claude Code, OpenCode and Windsurf, which start a project's
            servers in the project directory and document no editor variables, get <code>.</code> and their own
            spelling of <code>HOME</code>.
          </p>

          <h4 class="arch-subheading">4. What a new teammate sees: explain</h4>
          <p>
            Someone joining the team runs one command to learn what their assistant is given here and
            what they still have to do:
          </p>
          <pre><code>{explainOutput}</code></pre>

          <h4 class="arch-subheading">5. What CI checks: doctor and sync --check</h4>
          <pre><code>{doctorOutput}</code></pre>
          <p>
            The warning is <code>doctor</code> reading the team's own content: the deploy skill tells
            every agent to run a script and read a file that do not exist in this repository, and an agent
            would follow it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Now suppose someone adds a server called <code>scratchpad</code>, launched as
            <code>npx -y scratchpad-mcp</code>, to <em>shop</em>'s <code>mcpServers</code>. The baseline
            does not allow it and requires exact versions, so <code>sync</code> refuses and writes nothing:
          </p>
          <pre><code>{refusedOutput}</code></pre>
          <p>
            <code>sync --check</code> and <code>doctor</code> fail with the same two problems. A server
            added by hand to an MCP config is held to the same policy:
          </p>
          <pre><code>{handAddedOutput}</code></pre>

          <h4 class="arch-subheading">6. What a reviewer sees: review</h4>
          <p>
            A pull request moves the baseline from 1.4.0 to 1.5.0 — which adds an instruction, changes
            a skill and allows Sentry — and in the same change <em>shop</em> bumps its database server and
            adds <code>acme-flags</code>. Most of the diff is regenerated files in four assistants' formats.
            <code>opencastle review</code> writes this to the job summary instead:
          </p>
          <pre><code>{reviewOutput}</code></pre>

          <h4 class="arch-subheading">7. Across repositories: fleet</h4>
          <p>
            Acme's platform team checks where the new version has landed, from the locks committed in
            each checkout:
          </p>
          <pre><code>{fleetOutput}</code></pre>
