        </div>

        <!-- ═══════════ Config reference ═══════════ -->
        <section class="docs-section" id="config">
          <h3 class="section-title">Config Reference</h3>
          <p>
            <code>.opencastle/config.json</code> in a repository; <code>config.json</code> at the root of a
            baseline's layer. It is JSON with comments and trailing commas allowed. Point
            <code>$schema</code> at <a class="docs-link" href="/schema/config.json">https://www.opencastle.dev/schema/config.json</a>
            for completion in your editor. An unknown key is an error; when it is close to a real one, the
            message suggests the key you probably meant.
          </p>
          <pre><code>{fullConfig}</code></pre>

          <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
            <table class="comparison-table">
              <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>extends</code></td><td>string[]</td>
                  <td>Baselines to build on; the first entry is the lowest layer. Either a package name — installed as a devDependency and found in <code>node_modules</code> from this file's directory upwards, the way Node finds a package — or a path starting with <code>./</code> or <code>../</code>, relative to this file. A version here (<code>pkg@1.2.3</code>) is refused: it belongs in <code>package.json</code>, where your lockfile pins it. Absolute paths are refused too. A baseline's own <code>extends</code> load beneath it; one reached twice loads once; a cycle is an error.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>exclude</code></td><td>string[]</td>
                  <td>Items from the layers below to leave out, as <code>kind/name</code>: <code>skills/seo-patterns</code>, <code>agents/researcher</code> — or an MCP server a lower layer defines or an integration brings, as <code>mcpServers/Slack</code>. Naming content no lower layer provides is a warning. Excluding an item a lower layer's policy requires is an error.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>mcpServers</code></td><td>object</td>
                  <td>Servers every assistant gets, keyed by name (letters, digits, <code>.</code>, <code>_</code> and <code>-</code>). A layer above can redefine a server by name, and a team server named like an integration's replaces it. <code>sync</code> writes them exactly as defined, rewrites an entry someone edited in a target's MCP config, and removes one an earlier sync wrote that no layer defines any more.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>…command</code>, <code>args</code>, <code>env</code></td><td>string, string[], object</td>
                  <td>A local server: what to run, its arguments, and its environment.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>…url</code>, <code>headers</code></td><td>string, object</td>
                  <td>A remote server: its http(s) URL and the headers to send. A server has either <code>command</code> or <code>url</code>, never both.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>…type</code>, <code>description</code></td><td>string</td>
                  <td><code>type</code> is <code>stdio</code> or <code>http</code>, inferred from <code>command</code> or <code>url</code> when left out. <code>description</code> is for people reading the config; it is not written to the assistants' files.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>policy.mcp.allow</code></td><td>string[]</td>
                  <td>Server names an assistant may start; <code>*</code> matches any run of characters. See <a class="docs-link" href="#policy">Policy</a>.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>policy.mcp.remoteHosts</code></td><td>string[]</td>
                  <td>Hosts remote servers may connect to. <code>*.acme.dev</code> matches any subdomain of acme.dev, not acme.dev itself; anything else must match exactly.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>policy.mcp.requirePinned</code></td><td>boolean</td>
                  <td>Every server launched through a package runner (<code>npx</code>, <code>npm exec</code>, <code>pnpm dlx</code>, <code>yarn dlx</code>, <code>bunx</code>, <code>uvx</code>, <code>pipx run</code>) names an exact version, or runs the project's own copy with <code>npx --no</code>.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>policy.require</code></td><td>string[]</td>
                  <td>Items, as <code>kind/name</code>, that must be present. One that existed at or below the requiring layer may not be excluded or replaced above it; one that did not can be provided by any layer above.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>policy.contextBudget</code></td><td>integer</td>
                  <td>Tokens every assistant may load before it reads the task: the always-loaded instructions plus the index of skills and agents, estimated at four characters per token.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>opencastle</code></td><td>string</td>
                  <td>The OpenCastle versions this layer works with, as a semver range: exact, <code>^</code>, <code>~</code>, <code>1.0.x</code>, comparators such as <code>{'>=1.0.0 <1.3.0'}</code>, and <code>||</code>. Every layer's range must hold, or nothing compiles.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>$schema</code></td><td>string</td>
                  <td>The published schema, for your editor. Ignored when compiling.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            <strong>Variables.</strong> Write every variable as <code>{'${NAME}'}</code> in <code>env</code>,
            <code>headers</code>, <code>args</code> or <code>url</code>; <code>{'${env:NAME}'}</code> and
            <code>{'{env:NAME}'}</code> are read as the same thing. Keep the values in <code>.env</code> or
            your shell. <code>explain</code> lists every variable the servers read and whether you have set it.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════ Policy ═══════════ -->
        <section class="docs-section" id="policy">
          <h3 class="section-title">Policy</h3>
          <p>
            Policy is how the owners of a baseline make a standard hold in every repository that
            extends it. Any layer can add to it; no layer can loosen what a layer below set.
          </p>
          <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
            <table class="comparison-table">
              <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>How layers combine</th><th>When it is broken</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>mcp.allow</code></td>
                  <td>A server must be on every layer's list. Allowing a server a lower layer refuses has no effect, and says so.</td>
                  <td>A team server refused by its own layer or one below: compile error. Refused by a layer <em>above</em> the one that defines it — a repository narrowing its baseline's list — or an integration's server: left out of every assistant, with the reason in the lock and in <code>explain</code>. Any other server in an MCP config, such as one added by hand: <code>doctor</code> and <code>sync --check</code> fail.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>mcp.remoteHosts</code></td>
                  <td>A remote server's host must be on every layer's list.</td>
                  <td>As for <code>allow</code>.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>mcp.requirePinned</code></td>
                  <td>Once a layer turns it on, it stays on; <code>false</code> above it has no effect, and says so.</td>
                  <td>A team server: compile error. A server in an MCP config: <code>doctor</code> and <code>sync --check</code> fail. Without the rule, an unpinned server is only a warning.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>require</code></td>
                  <td>Accumulates.</td>
                  <td>Excluding a required item, or replacing one that existed where it was required: compile error. Providing one nothing below had is how it is meant to be met.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>contextBudget</code></td>
                  <td>The smallest wins.</td>
                  <td>Not an error. <code>doctor</code> warns and names the largest contributors; <code>review</code> marks the change ⚠️.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><code>opencastle</code></td>
                  <td>Every layer's range must hold.</td>
                  <td>Compile error.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            <strong>Credentials</strong> need no policy. A team server with a credential written into it —
            a value in <code>env</code>, <code>headers</code>, <code>args</code> or the URL shaped like a real
            token, or a long random value under a name such as <code>TOKEN</code> or <code>API_KEY</code> — is a
            compile error, and the same in any MCP config fails <code>doctor</code> and <code>sync --check</code>.
            The message names where the credential is, never its value.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A compile error</strong> means <code>sync</code> writes nothing and says why;
            <code>sync --check</code> and <code>doctor</code> fail with the same message; <code>review</code>
            and <code>explain</code> refuse, because there is nothing coherent to describe.
          </p>
          <div class="key-insight">
            <p><strong>Why some servers are left out rather than refused:</strong> your team chose the policy, and an integration's skill is still useful without its server. A server a layer defines against its own rules, or rules below it, is a mistake someone made in the config, so it stops the build. A layer above that refuses it is opting out, so the server is left out — as <code>exclude: ["mcpServers/&lt;name&gt;"]</code> does explicitly.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════ Lock ═══════════ -->
        <section class="docs-section" id="lock">
          <h3 class="section-title">The Lock File</h3>
          <p>
            <code>sync</code> writes <code>.opencastle/lock.json</code>. Commit it. One change to a baseline
            regenerates dozens of files across several assistants, and nobody reviews that; the lock is the
            one file that says, in a shape a person can read, what every assistant is given.
          </p>
          <ul class="docs-list">
            <li><strong>Layers</strong> — each with its version, and for a baseline a digest of its content, so a baseline that changed without a version bump still shows.</li>
            <li><strong>Content</strong> — every instruction, skill, agent, prompt and workflow, with the layer it came from (or the integration, as <code>plugin:&lt;id&gt;</code>), a hash of what was compiled, what it replaced, its description, and for instructions an estimate of their tokens.</li>
            <li><strong>MCP servers</strong> — each with where it came from, whether it is local or remote, the command line or URL, and the names of the variables it reads. Integration servers the policy refused are listed under <code>blocked</code>, with why.</li>
            <li><strong>Policy</strong> — the effective rules, and which layer set each.</li>
            <li><strong>Context</strong> — the tokens every assistant loads before a task.</li>
          </ul>
          <pre><code>{lockExcerpt}</code></pre>
          <p>
            It is deterministic — no timestamps, no absolute paths, keys in a fixed order — so it changes only
            when what the assistants get changes, and two branches conflict on it only when they disagree.
            <code>sync --check</code> fails when it is missing or stale. Do not edit it: change
            <code>.opencastle/</code> or the baseline and run <code>sync</code>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every change to what an assistant is given moves the lock, so owning the lock routes all of them to
            the right reviewers. <code>opencastle ci --owners @acme/platform</code> appends this to
            <code>.github/CODEOWNERS</code>:
          </p>
          <pre><code>{codeowners}</code></pre>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════ Team memory ═══════════ -->
        <section class="docs-section" id="memory">
          <h3 class="section-title">Team Memory</h3>
          <p>
            What agents learn on a project — a command that fails, a convention written down nowhere else —
            is worth more to the team than to the session that learned it. OpenCastle keeps it in three places,
            from the narrowest to the widest, and moves it up on purpose, never on its own.
          </p>
          <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
            <table class="comparison-table">
              <thead><tr><th>Scope</th><th>Where it lives</th><th>Who it reaches</th><th>Moves up with</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Personal</td><td>Your assistant's own: Claude Code's auto memory, <code>~/.claude/skills</code>, <code>~/.agents/skills</code></td><td>You, on one machine</td><td><code>opencastle promote memory</code>, <code>promote skill</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Team</td><td><code>.opencastle/lessons/</code> and <code>.opencastle/skills/</code>, committed</td><td>Every assistant of everyone in the repository</td><td><code>promote skill --to &lt;baseline&gt;</code>; the <strong>memory-merger</strong> skill turns mature lessons into skills and instructions</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Organisation</td><td>A baseline — an <a href="/docs/agent-plugins">Agent Plugin</a> every repository extends</td><td>Every repository</td><td>—</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            <strong>Lessons</strong> are one file each, written by <code>opencastle lesson</code>, so two branches that
            each add one merge cleanly; <code>.opencastle/LESSONS-LEARNED.md</code> is the index agents read first,
            rebuilt by <code>sync</code> and checked by <code>sync --check</code>. A lesson can cite the code it is
            about with <code>--cite</code>. <code>doctor</code> then names any lesson whose cited file has changed or gone
            since it was verified, and the fix — <code>lesson verify</code> when it still holds, <code>lesson archive</code>
            when it has been merged into a skill — so a stale lesson does not keep steering agents.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Promotion</strong> is how knowledge leaves one laptop. <code>promote skill</code> copies a skill you
            wrote for your own assistant into the team's sources, after checking it against the Agent Skills spec and
            for credentials. <code>promote memory</code> turns what Claude Code remembered in this repository into lessons:
          </p>
          <pre><code>{promoteOutput}</code></pre>
          <p>
            Corrections and project notes become lessons; memories about you, ones holding a credential, and ones
            already promoted are left out, and your home directory is written as <code>~</code>. Before you commit,
            set the category and severity and cite the code:
          </p>
          <pre><code>{lessonFile}</code></pre>
          <p>
            Nothing is shared until it is committed. The pull request that commits a promoted skill or lesson is where
            the team agrees with it — and, with CODEOWNERS on <code>.opencastle/</code>, where its owners do.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════ Team commands ═══════════ -->
        <section class="docs-section" id="commands">
          <h3 class="section-title">Team Commands</h3>
          <p>
            Seven commands, each described in full in the <a class="docs-link" href="/docs/cli#teams">CLI reference</a>.
          </p>

          <h4 class="arch-subheading">explain</h4>
          <pre><code>npx opencastle explain [--all] [--json]</code></pre>
          <p>
            What every assistant here is given: the layers, what is always loaded and what it costs, the team's
            skills, agents, prompts and workflows by origin (<code>--all</code> lists OpenCastle's own too), the
            MCP servers, the policy, and under <em>Your setup</em> the variables you have not set and the servers
            that will ask you to sign in. A variable counts as set when it is in your shell or in <code>.env</code>
            at the project root.
          </p>

          <h4 class="arch-subheading">review</h4>
          <pre><code>npx opencastle review [--base &lt;ref&gt;] [--markdown] [--json]</code></pre>
          <p>
            Compares the lock committed at <code>--base</code> (default <code>HEAD</code>; on a GitHub Actions pull
            request, <code>origin/&lt;base branch&gt;</code>) with what the current sources compile to, and says what
            changed in sentences. It marks ⚠️: a baseline added, dropped, or changed without a version change; a new
            or changed always-loaded instruction; a required item that moved or went; every new MCP server, and one
            that now launches differently or reads different variables; a widened or removed allowlist or host list;
            pinning or a requirement dropped; the budget removed; and context growth over 10% or past the
            budget. On GitHub Actions it also appends the Markdown to the job summary. It exits 0 whatever it finds —
            <code>sync --check</code> is the gate — and 1 only when it cannot compare, for instance when the base is
            not a commit here; in CI, check out with <code>fetch-depth: 0</code>. If the committed lock is stale, it
            says so first.
          </p>

          <h4 class="arch-subheading">ci</h4>
          <pre><code>npx opencastle ci [--owners &lt;team&gt;] [--force] [--dry-run]</code></pre>
          <p>
            Writes <code>.github/workflows/opencastle.yml</code>. For <em>shop</em>, <code>--dry-run</code> shows this
            workflow:
          </p>
          <pre><code>{ciWorkflow}</code></pre>
          <p>
            It installs dependencies with the package manager your lockfile names (npm, pnpm, Yarn or Bun), so
            baselines resolve. When <code>opencastle</code> is one of your devDependencies it runs that copy —
            <code>npx --no opencastle</code>, <code>pnpm exec opencastle</code> or <code>yarn opencastle</code> —
            so laptops and CI run the version your lockfile pins. Otherwise, as here, it pins the release that last
            compiled the project and tells you to add the devDependency. Run it from a subdirectory of a monorepo and
            the workflow is named after that directory, filtered to its paths, and runs there. An existing workflow is
            replaced only with <code>--force</code>. <code>--owners</code> appends to the CODEOWNERS file GitHub already
            reads — <code>.github/</code>, the root or <code>docs/</code>, in that order — and creates
            <code>.github/CODEOWNERS</code> only when there is none, since GitHub reads the first it finds; it leaves the
            file alone if it already routes the lock.
          </p>

          <h4 class="arch-subheading">baseline</h4>
          <pre><code>npx opencastle baseline &lt;init|check&gt; [dir] [--name &lt;pkg&gt;] [--json]</code></pre>
          <p>
            <code>init</code> scaffolds a baseline package in <code>./opencastle-baseline</code> or the directory you
            name, laid out as an Agent Plugin: <code>plugin.json</code>, an example skill in <code>skills/</code>,
            <code>mcp.json</code>, an example instruction and the policy in <code>dev.opencastle/</code>, Claude Code's
            <code>.claude-plugin/plugin.json</code>, a <code>package.json</code> that declares the layer and ships all of it
            in <code>"files"</code>, a README on publishing and adopting it, and a CI workflow that runs <code>check</code>. It refuses a directory that already has a
            <code>package.json</code>. <code>check</code> resolves the baseline exactly as a repository extending it
            would and reports what it contributes and how many tokens load up front. It fails — exit 1 — when the
            package does not declare <code>{'"opencastle": { "baseline": "<dir>" }'}</code>, when <code>"files"</code>
            would leave the layer out of <code>npm publish</code>, or when the config, content or policy has an error.
          </p>
          <pre><code>{baselineCheckOutput}</code></pre>

          <h4 class="arch-subheading">plugin</h4>
          <pre><code>npx opencastle plugin &lt;check|build|index&gt; [dir] [--check] [--name &lt;name&gt;] [--owner &lt;name&gt;] [--json]</code></pre>
          <p>
            <code>check</code> loads an Agent Plugin the way an assistant that follows the standard does and says what it
            would refuse or skip; <code>build</code> writes the manifest and MCP file Claude Code reads instead of the
            portable ones; <code>index</code> writes the marketplace files Claude Code, Copilot, Cursor and Codex read for a
            repository of plugins. See <a href="/docs/agent-plugins">Agent Plugins</a>.
          </p>

          <h4 class="arch-subheading">promote</h4>
          <pre><code>npx opencastle promote &lt;skill|memory&gt; [name|path] [--to &lt;dir&gt;] [--from &lt;dir&gt;] [--force] [--dry-run]</code></pre>
          <p>
            Copies a skill from your own skill directories into <code>.opencastle/skills/</code> or a baseline, or turns
            Claude Code's auto memory for the repository into lessons. See <a href="#memory">Team Memory</a>.
          </p>

          <h4 class="arch-subheading">fleet</h4>
          <pre><code>npx opencastle fleet &lt;dir...&gt; [--json]</code></pre>
          <p>
            Reads the committed <code>.opencastle/lock.json</code> of each directory — nothing is installed or
            fetched — and shows which OpenCastle and baseline versions each repository runs, which lag behind the
            newest, and which MCP servers launch differently across them. Directories without
            <code>.opencastle/</code> are skipped; one without a lock is listed as not yet synced by a release that
            writes one, and one whose lock this release cannot read — written by a newer OpenCastle, or edited — is
            listed as such.
          </p>

          <h4 class="arch-subheading">And what changed in doctor and sync</h4>
          <p>
            <code>doctor</code> now also reports whether the team sources resolve; the always-loaded context
            against the budget, naming the three largest contributors when it is over; <code>npm run</code> scripts
            named anywhere in team content, and backticked paths named in the repository's own content, that no longer
            exist; and an OpenCastle older than the one that compiled the project. <code>sync</code> refuses to
            compile with an older release than the one that last compiled the project — it would replace newer output
            with older, and the next teammate's sync would put it back — unless you pass
            <code>--allow-downgrade</code>. <code>sync --check</code> run by an older release says so and compares
            nothing, instead of reporting every file as drifted.
          </p>
        </section>

