        <!-- ═══════════ Rollout ═══════════ -->
        <section class="docs-section" id="rollout">
          <h3 class="section-title">Rollout Recipe</h3>
          <ol class="steps">
            <li>
              <strong>Create the baseline.</strong> <code>npx opencastle baseline init --name @acme/opencastle-baseline</code>.
              Move the rules every repository shares into <code>dev.opencastle/instructions/</code> and keep them short — every
              token there loads before every task in every repository. Put detail in <code>skills/</code>, which
              loads only when a task needs it. Set the policy in <code>dev.opencastle/config.json</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Check and publish.</strong> <code>npx opencastle baseline check</code> validates it the way every
              repository will read it; the scaffolded workflow runs the same check on every pull request. Publish it to
              your registry like any other package.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Adopt it in each repository.</strong> <code>npm i -D @acme/opencastle-baseline opencastle</code>,
              add <code>{'"extends": ["@acme/opencastle-baseline"]'}</code> to <code>.opencastle/config.json</code>,
              run <code>npx opencastle sync</code>, and commit the generated files and the lock. Having
              <code>opencastle</code> in devDependencies means everyone runs one version.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Wire up CI.</strong> <code>npx opencastle ci --owners @acme/platform</code>, then commit the
              workflow and <code>.github/CODEOWNERS</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Let your upgrade bot roll out new versions.</strong> When you publish a new baseline, Renovate or
              Dependabot opens one pull request per repository. The bot changes only <code>package.json</code> and your
              lockfile, so <code>sync --check</code> fails on its pull request until someone runs
              <code>npx opencastle sync</code> on the branch and pushes. That is the point: the review summary on the
              same pull request says what the new version changes in that repository before anyone merges it.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Review in pull requests.</strong> Read the ⚠️ lines closely: a new MCP server, a widened
              allowlist, a new always-loaded instruction. With CODEOWNERS in place, the owners approve every change to
              what the assistants are given.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Watch the fleet.</strong> <code>npx opencastle fleet ~/src/*</code> over your checkouts shows who
              is behind and which servers run at different versions.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════ Limits ═══════════ -->
        <section class="docs-section" id="limits">
          <h3 class="section-title">Limits</h3>
          <ul class="docs-list">
            <li><strong>OpenCastle fetches nothing.</strong> A baseline must be installed where Node would find it — npm, pnpm, Yarn and workspaces all work, and so does a git dependency your package manager installs. CI must install dependencies before it runs OpenCastle; the workflow <code>ci</code> writes does.</li>
            <li><strong>GitHub Actions only, for now.</strong> <code>ci</code> writes a GitHub Actions workflow, and the annotations and job summary are GitHub's. Elsewhere, run <code>sync --check</code> and <code>review --markdown</code> in your own pipeline. <code>review</code> writes to the job summary, not a pull request comment.</li>
            <li><strong><code>fleet</code> reads checkouts.</strong> There is no hosted view; you point it at directories you have cloned.</li>
            <li><strong>The context figure is an estimate.</strong> It is one number — four characters per token over the merged instructions and the skill and agent index — not a measurement of what each assistant loads.</li>
            <li><strong>A pin fixes a server's own version,</strong> not its dependencies, which <code>npx</code> resolves on each machine. Launching the project's own copy with <code>npx --no</code>, or a remote server, avoids that.</li>
            <li><strong>Windsurf and Antigravity are less certain.</strong> Antigravity receives <code>{'${NAME}'}</code> until its variable syntax is confirmed, and Windsurf's MCP config is documented as one global file — so team servers may not reach those assistants yet. Codex CLI gets its servers in <code>.codex/config.toml</code>, which it loads only in a project you have marked as trusted.</li>
            <li><strong>Replacing is all or nothing.</strong> A layer that provides an item with the same name replaces the whole item; two layers' versions of one skill are never merged.</li>
            <li><strong>Policy covers what is present, not what it says.</strong> It decides which servers run and which items must exist; it does not read the text of instructions.</li>
            <li><strong>Dead references are checked narrowly.</strong> Scripts named as <code>npm run</code> (or <code>pnpm</code>, <code>yarn</code>, <code>bun run</code>) are checked in all team content; backticked paths only in the repository's own content, and only under a top-level directory that exists.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            What is still open, and why: <a class="docs-link" href="https://github.com/monkilabs/opencastle/blob/main/docs/teams.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">docs/teams.md</a>.
          </p>
        </section>
      </main>
